1
00:00:01,033 --> 00:00:03,605
This video is an introduction
to the course,

2
00:00:03,605 --> 00:00:05,916
Advanced Embedded Linux Development.

3
00:00:05,916 --> 00:00:07,994
So we're going to do
an introduction to the course.

4
00:00:07,994 --> 00:00:10,526
Talk about why you'd
bother to learn Linux, and

5
00:00:10,526 --> 00:00:12,767
talk about what this course is and isn't.

6
00:00:14,036 --> 00:00:16,713
So, first of all, who is this guy?

7
00:00:16,713 --> 00:00:22,929
My name is Dan Walkes and I currently work
full time in industry and teach part-time.

8
00:00:22,929 --> 00:00:27,229
My current gig is Vice President of
Embedded Engineering at Sighthound.

9
00:00:27,229 --> 00:00:31,556
I began teaching at CU in
the spring 2019 semester, and

10
00:00:31,556 --> 00:00:37,191
I'm a former CU Boulder Embedded Systems
student from the early 2000s.

11
00:00:37,191 --> 00:00:41,857
And during that time, I was ta for
ECEN 5623, real time embedded.

12
00:00:41,857 --> 00:00:45,908
So I've spent quite a bit of time
learning about embedded systems, and

13
00:00:45,908 --> 00:00:49,620
it's been a great pleasure of mind
to put this course together and

14
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:51,727
to be able to share it with all of you.

15
00:00:53,269 --> 00:00:58,398
So my 20th year of industry experience
was in 2019, and most of my

16
00:00:58,398 --> 00:01:04,470
of the previous 12 or 10 or 12 years have
been Linux embedded systems related.

17
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:06,369
I am not a doctor and I'm not a professor.

18
00:01:06,369 --> 00:01:08,347
So you can refer to me as Dan.

19
00:01:08,347 --> 00:01:11,108
And the attitude that
I try to approach this

20
00:01:11,108 --> 00:01:15,879
course with is really kind of like as
a team lead on your development team.

21
00:01:15,879 --> 00:01:20,055
So if you can think of me in that respect.

22
00:01:20,055 --> 00:01:22,581
So talking about why
should we learned Linux?

23
00:01:22,581 --> 00:01:27,540
There's a EETimes Embedded Market Study
from 2019

24
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:32,394
that kind of showed the responses
from folks working as

25
00:01:32,394 --> 00:01:36,826
electrical engineers and
what they were using for

26
00:01:36,826 --> 00:01:41,909
their operating system in
their development projects.

27
00:01:41,909 --> 00:01:46,877
And if you bundle a bunch of the different
Linux based variants together, and

28
00:01:46,877 --> 00:01:51,845
if you include platforms like android
that are based on Linux, what you see is

29
00:01:51,845 --> 00:01:56,662
that a huge percent percentage of
respondents are either actively using or

30
00:01:56,662 --> 00:02:00,040
planning to use Linux on
projects in the short term.

31
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:03,150
So it's one of the most popular
embedded systems out there.

32
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:05,479
It's one that if you
know something about it,

33
00:02:05,479 --> 00:02:09,313
there's a pretty good chance you're
going to be able to apply it in your work.

34
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:13,430
So what do I want to do with this course?

35
00:02:13,430 --> 00:02:17,354
I want to give you the tools that you
need to understand how to configure and

36
00:02:17,354 --> 00:02:19,489
deploy Linux based embedded systems.

37
00:02:19,489 --> 00:02:23,098
And so, I want to give you some experience
with some of the common system build tools

38
00:02:23,098 --> 00:02:23,898
and components.

39
00:02:23,898 --> 00:02:26,514
So that will include BASH and
shell scripting.

40
00:02:26,514 --> 00:02:28,192
It will include Buildroot and

41
00:02:28,192 --> 00:02:32,431
Yocto as build frameworks to build
your own embedded Linux distribution.

42
00:02:32,431 --> 00:02:39,284
I use QEMU for virtualization for
emulation of your embedded environment.

43
00:02:41,734 --> 00:02:46,690
So I want to make sure that this course
is about gaining experience with Linux

44
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:51,411
driver development in the follow-on
portion of the course and general

45
00:02:51,411 --> 00:02:56,539
improvement in your resume interview
skills in relation to embedded Linux.

46
00:02:56,539 --> 00:03:00,546
And really the part of the reason I
developed this course is there's a lot of

47
00:03:00,546 --> 00:03:04,614
things in here that I wish I would have
had as part of my graduate studies, and

48
00:03:04,614 --> 00:03:07,183
in a lot of cases they
didn't really exist yet.

49
00:03:07,183 --> 00:03:10,923
So, especially some of the build
frameworks that we'll talk about here

50
00:03:10,923 --> 00:03:11,793
in this course.

51
00:03:11,793 --> 00:03:14,878
So it might be important to talk a little
bit about what this course is not.

52
00:03:14,878 --> 00:03:18,253
It's not really data structures or
an algorithms course.

53
00:03:18,253 --> 00:03:22,499
So we might cover a little bit about
data structures or algorithms, but

54
00:03:22,499 --> 00:03:25,614
it's mostly about how to
use existing libraries and

55
00:03:25,614 --> 00:03:28,189
software in the case
that we do cover them.

56
00:03:28,189 --> 00:03:32,919
It's also not really about low resource
microcontroller or anything like FreeRTOS

57
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:36,789
is not going to cover event loops or
anything like Bluetooth protocol.

58
00:03:36,789 --> 00:03:41,652
We are not specifically covering sensors,
although in the following portion of

59
00:03:41,652 --> 00:03:45,655
this course where you do a final project,
you'll be able to select

60
00:03:45,655 --> 00:03:49,604
a sensor potentially to integrate
as part of your final project.

61
00:03:49,604 --> 00:03:53,960
And we're not really covering embedded
libraries for low resource processors.

62
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:57,111
It'll all just be about Linux and
Linux application development.